            This is required to be universal in the sense that given every such diagram as above with domain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to L
            making the diagrams commute.
          %p
            It is clear that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is the constant functor sending everything to a singleton set, this recovers the usual notion of limit for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %h2#motivation_from_enriched_category_theory Motivation from enriched category theory
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %p
            Imagine you’re tasked to write down the definition of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon K \to C
            and a limit is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            over it, i.e. it’s the universal choice of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            together with an arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_k \colon c \to F(k)
            for each object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            Here’s where you stop and ask yourself: what is ‘an arrow’ in
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has no
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
            — it has
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-objects"} hom-objects
            — hence what’s ‘an element’ of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c, F(k))
            in
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            There are two ways to specify an element of an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, I, \otimes)
          %ol
            %li
              Give an arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to X
              (think of sets, where elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              are indeed the same thing as arrows
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{*\} \to X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+elements"} global elements
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is ‘too big’ to thoroughly probe
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (on the other hand, notice the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category#passage_between_ordinary_categories_and_enriched_categories"} underlying category of an enriched category
              is defined by taking global elements of the hom-objects)
            %li
              Give
              %em any
              arrow into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+elements"} generalized elements
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
              assures us they completely capture the categorical structure of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Hence you now say: a cone over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a choice of a
            %em generalized element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_k
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c, F(k))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_k \to C(c, F(k))
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            as an element
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [K, V](W(-), C(c, F(-)))
          %p
            Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is simply a uniform way to specify the sides of a cone. A confirmation that this is indeed the right definition of limit in the enriched settings come from the fact that conical completeness (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+limit"} conical limit
            is one where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W = \Delta I
            %a{:href => "#Kelly"} Kelly’s book
            (aptly named
            = succeed ")." do
              %em The inadequacy of conical limits
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#homotopy_limits Homotopy limits
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            some category of higher structures, the
            %em local
            definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            over a diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : K \to C
            replaces the ordinary notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            by a higher cone in which all triangles of 1-morphisms are filled by 2-cells, all tetrahedra by 3-cells, etc.
          %p
            One can convince oneself that for the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SimpSet
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            this is realized in terms of the weighted limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi lim
                    %mi W
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim^W F
            with the weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            taken to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W : K \to \Simp\Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo :
                  %mi k
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W : k \mapsto N(K/k)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K/k
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} over category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(K/k)
            denotes its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
          %p
            This leads to the classical definition of homotopy limits in
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Simp\Set
          %ul
            %li
              A.K. Bousfield and D.M. Kan,
              %em Homotopy limits, completions, and localizations
          %p See for instance also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marc+Cordier"} Jean-Marc Cordier
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Timothy+Porter"} Timothy Porter
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy Coherent Category Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/tran/1997-349-01/S0002-9947-97-01752-2/S0002-9947-97-01752-2.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Gambino"}> Nicola Gambino
                ,
                %em Weighted limits in simplicial homotopy theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.crm.cat/Publications/08/Pr790.pdf"} pdf
                or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.unipa.it/%7Engambino/Research/Papers/weighted.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            In some nice cases the weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(K/-)
            can be replaced by a simpler weight; an example is discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield-Kan+map"} Bousfield-Kan map
          %h3#homotopy_pullback Homotopy pullback
          %p
            For instance in the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                  %mo ←
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K = \{r \to t \leftarrow s\}
            is the shape of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            diagrams we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W(r) = \{r\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W(s) = \{s\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                  %mo ←
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W(t) = N( \{r \to t \leftarrow s\} )
          %p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                  %mo ←
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(r \to t) : \{r\} \to \{r \to t \leftarrow s\}
            injects the vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi r
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                  %mo ←
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{r \to t \leftarrow s\}
            and similarly for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo →
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(s \to t)
          %p
            This implies that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : K \to C
            a pullback diagram in the
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SimpSet
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with tip some object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c  \in C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W \Rightarrow C(c, F(-))
          %p is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                a “morphism” from the tip
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(r)
                (i.e. a vertex in the Hom-simplicial set
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c,F(r))
            %li
              %p
                similarly over
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %li
              %p
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                three “morphisms” from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(t)
                together with 2-cells between them (i.e. a 2-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
                in the Hom-simplicial set
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c,F(t))
            %li
              %p
                such that the two outer morphisms over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                are identified with the morphisms over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo →
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(r \to t)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo →
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(s \to t)
          %p
            So in total such a
            = succeed "-weighted" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇒
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇐
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi r
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi r
                              %mo →
                              %mi t
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi s
                              %mo →
                              %mi t
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp; c
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;\Rightarrow&amp; \downarrow
                  \&amp;\Leftarrow&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  F(r)
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\stackrel{F(r \to t)}{\to}
                  \&amp;
                  F(t)
                  \&amp;
                  \\stackrel{F(s \to t)}{\leftarrow}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  F(s)
                  }
          %p as one would expect for a “homotopy cone”.
          %h1#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-limit"} strict 2-limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/saturated+class+of+limits"} saturated class of limits
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+colimit"} weighted colimit
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The notion of weighted limits was introduced (under the name “
            %em> mean cotensor product
            ”) in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , p. 10 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Une notion de
                    = succeed "-limite" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi V
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Colloque sur l’algèbre des catégories. Amiens 1973. Résumés des conférences
                %strong 14
                2 (1973) 153-223 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1973__14_2_153_0"}> numdam:CTGDC_1973__14_2_153_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Maxwell+Kelly"} Gregory Maxwell Kelly
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A notion of limit for enriched categories
                %strong 12
                1 (1975) 49-72 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0004972700023637"}> doi:10.1017/S0004972700023637
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            and, independently, (under the name “
            %em> Hom (formel)
            ”) by:
          %ul
            %li
              C. Auderset,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjonction et monade au niveau des 2-categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1974__15_1_3_0/"} numdam
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Kelly
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
                ,
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/10/tr10.pdf#page=37"} section 3.1, p. 37
                in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Basic concepts of enriched category theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 64
                %strong 10
                (2005) 1-136 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/academic/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/basic-concepts-enriched-category-theory?format=PB&isbn=9780521287029"}> ISBN:9780521287029
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10abs.html"} tac:tr10
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , §6.6 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories and Structures
                %strong 50
                Cambridge University Press (1994) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525865"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525865
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              ,
              %em Weighted limits and colimits
              (2008) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/weighted.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            is given an account of lectures by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
            on the subject. The definition appears there as
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/weighted.pdf#page=4"} definition 3.1, p. 4
            (in a form a bit more general than the one above).
          %h3#presenting_homotopy_limits Presenting homotopy limits
          %p
            On weighted limits as presentations of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limits"} homotopy limits
          %ul
            %li#Hirschhorn02
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Hirschhorn"}> Philip Hirschhorn
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Model+Categories+and+Their+Localizations"} Model Categories and Their Localizations
              %strong 99
              (2002) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-99-s/"}> ISBN:978-0-8218-4917-0
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.gbv.de/dms/goettingen/360115845.pdf"} pdf toc
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://people.math.rochester.edu/faculty/doug/otherpapers/pshmain.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/hirschhornloc.pdf"} pdf
          %p To compare with the above discussion notice that
          %ul
            %li
              %p The functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      W \;\coloneqq\; N(K/-)
              %p is discussed there in definition 14.7.8 on p. 269.
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi K
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [K,V]
                which on
                = succeed "-functors" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S,T
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi K
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi K
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [K,V](S,T) = \int_{k \in K} V(S(k), T(k))
                appears as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi hom
                        %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom^K(S,T)
                in definition 18.3.1 (see bottom of the page).
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                set to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SimpSet
                the above definition of homotopy limit appears in example 18.3.6 (2).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                , §6.6 and Chapter 7 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+Homotopy+Theory"} Categorical Homotopy Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107261457"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781107261457
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/cathtpy.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion of weighted
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-limit"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                \-limits
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martina+Rovelli"}> Martina Rovelli
              ,
              %em Weighted limits in an (∞,1)-category
              (2019) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00805"}> arxiv:1902.00805
              \&amp;rbrack;
